From: tcmay@netcom.com (Timothy C. May)
Subject: Why We Should _All_ Admire Scrooge

> Agreed. Like 'Bladerunner', this ubiquitous story of Mr. Dickens gets much
> more enjoyable if you treat the nominal villain as a hero and the nominal
> comedy as a tragedy. Try to watch the old black and white film (sorry,
> I don't remember the year or the cast) and convince yourself that
> Ayn Rand did *not* write Scrooge's lines. After a while, it's pretty
> hard to do.
> 
> Elias Israel

Yes. The version I like is the one with Alistair Sim, circa the late
1930s or early 1940s. Perhaps this is the version Elias is thinking
of. 

It appears every year on t.v., usually late at night (and perhaps
being displaced by the even sappier "It's a Wonderful Life," which
features an evil mortgage-holder who threatens to foreclose just
because the money he lent out isn't being repaid).

Personally, I would give Xmas vacation, Xmas parties, etc., to my
employees (if I had any). Just a part of doing business, harmless fun,
etc. Part of the voluntary deal made....the employees work hard most
of the time and get to goof off at office parties, Halloween dress-up
days, and Xmas. No big deal.

Scrooge is just a place-holder for---drum-roll---Scrooge-like behavior
(which points to Dickens' genius as a polemicist/propagandist, that we
use his place-holders as basic memes....like Rand). I don't claim to
emulate all the characteristics of Scrooge, only to argue it is my
"right" (won't expand on this issue here) to act Scrooge-like if I
please. Even Dickens did not dispute this, or call for government
regulation of Xmas spirit....rather, he invoked personal psychological
demons (long before Freud) in the dreams Scrooge had.

But calling people Scrooges or Nazis on this List is a very poor
debating tactic. Those, like Sandy Sandfort, who oh-so-reasonably ask
why we can't all just get along, would do well to read the posts of
Fabijanic, Starr, and others and see who lowered the level of the
debate.

(I count Sandy as a friend, so I am just pointing out that "why can't
we all just get along?" sentiments are fine, in the abstract, but
specific situations often make conflict inevitable. And when attacked
as a Nazi, or a Scrooge, or as "not moderate enough," I will fight
back. Think of it as tit for tat.)

--Tim May



